1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,814
In 2038, some computers will think it's 1901.

2
00:00:03,814 --> 00:00:07,651
They count time in seconds since January 1st, 1970.

3
00:00:07,651 --> 00:00:10,873
And many store that count in a signed 32-bit number.

4
00:00:10,873 --> 00:00:13,627
Its limit: about 2.1 billion seconds.

5
00:00:13,627 --> 00:00:16,707
That runs out on January 19th, 2038.

6
00:00:16,707 --> 00:00:18,920
One second later, the top bit flips.

7
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:21,050
The number becomes hugely negative.

8
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:23,396
And the clock jumps back to 1901.

9
00:00:23,396 --> 00:00:29,316
Most modern systems moved to 64-bit time, good for 292 billion years.

10
00:00:29,316 --> 00:00:33,708
But old devices and forgotten code are still counting toward 2038.
